## What this is

A self-hosted deployment of the CDT platform. Everything runs from pre-built images on the
GitHub Container Registry — no source code, no build tooling, no Node.js on the host. The only
things you supply are Docker and a `.env` file.

The stack is defined in [`docker-compose.public.yml`](docker-compose.public.yml):

| Service | Image | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `postgres` | `postgis/postgis:15-3.4` | Relational + geospatial database |
| `minio` | `minio/minio` | Object storage for BIM, point clouds, GIS files |
| `minio-init` | `minio/mc` | One-shot: creates the `org-logos` bucket, then exits |
| `migrate` | `ghcr.io/…/cdt-migrate` | One-shot: runs `prisma migrate deploy`, then exits |
| `cdt` | `ghcr.io/…/cdt-image` | The platform itself — API and web UI |

The two one-shot services run on every startup and are safe to re-run: they skip buckets that
already exist and migrations that have already been applied. The `:latest` images documented
here currently ship `@collabdt/core` 0.8.2. `org-logos` is world-readable by design — logos are
served straight from it — so put nothing else in that bucket.

---

## Prerequisites

- **Docker Desktop** (Windows/macOS) or **Docker Engine + Compose** (Linux) —
  [install instructions](https://docs.docker.com/get-started/get-docker/). Podman Compose also
  works; the compose file already carries the `:Z` volume flags it needs.
- A `.env` file in this directory. Start from the
  [example](https://docs.collabdt.org/resources/self-hosting/env.example) and see
  [Environment Configuration](https://docs.collabdt.org/docs/deployment/self-hosting/#environment-configuration)
  for what each variable does.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Docker has to be running before any command below.** On Windows and macOS that means
> launching Docker Desktop and waiting until the whale icon reports **Engine running** — the
> daemon is not started by the CLI. If it isn't up you'll get:
>
> ```
> error during connect: ... The system cannot find the file specified.
> ```
>
> That message means Docker, not CDT. Start Docker Desktop and re-run.

---

## Start the stack

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml up -d
```

First run pulls several GB of images and initializes the database — give it a few minutes.
Watch it come up with:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml logs -f cdt
```

The app is ready when the log prints `Ready in …` and `docker compose ps` shows `cdt` as
`healthy`.

### Where to open it

| What | URL | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **CDT platform** | **http://localhost:6012** | The app. This is the one you want. |
| MinIO console | http://localhost:9001 | Browse stored files; log in with `MINIO_ROOT_USER` / `MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD` |
| MinIO API | http://localhost:9000 | Not for browsing — presigned upload/download URLs point here |
| PostgreSQL | `localhost:5433` | Mapped off 5432 so it won't collide with a local Postgres — see [Database access](#database-access) |

Port 6012 is fixed by the compose file (`6012:3000`). If you change it, change `AUTH_URL` and
`ALLOWED_ORIGIN` in `.env` to match — sign-in breaks otherwise.

---

## First run — create your organization

A fresh install has no organization and no users. Open the platform URL and you are redirected to
`/organization-config`, a one-time setup form that asks for:

- **Organization name** — 3–63 characters, lowercase letters, numbers, dots and hyphens (e.g. `my-org`)
- **Organization title and description** — what people see in the UI
- **Languages, viewers, and data types** — what to enable for this organization
- **Admin name, email, and password** — the first administrator account

Submitting takes you to the sign-in page. The setup form is reachable only while no organization
exists; afterwards it redirects to sign-in. Change the admin password after first login if the
credentials were shared with anyone during setup.

---

## Update to the latest release

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml pull
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml up -d
```

`pull` fetches the newest images; `up -d` recreates only the containers whose image changed and
leaves the rest running. Pending database migrations are applied automatically by the `migrate`
service before the app starts. Your data lives in named volumes and survives the upgrade.

---

## Changing environment variables

After editing `.env`, recreate the app container — `restart` is not enough:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml up -d
```

Environment variables are fixed when a container is *created*, so `docker compose restart cdt`
brings back the same container with the same stale values. Only `up -d` notices the changed config
and replaces it, recreating just what changed and leaving your data volumes alone. Verify with:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml exec cdt sh -c 'echo $YOUR_VARIABLE'
```

The `cdt` service loads the whole file via `env_file: .env`, so a new variable needs no compose
edit. The one-shot `migrate` service re-runs (safe — applied migrations are skipped), and if you
changed `AUTH_SECRET` every session cookie becomes undecryptable
(`JWTSessionError: no matching decryption secret`) until users clear cookies and sign in again.

---

## Everyday commands

```bash
# Status of every service
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml ps

# Follow logs (all services, or one)
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml logs -f
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml logs -f cdt

# Restart just the app (does not pick up .env changes — see "Changing environment variables")
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml restart cdt

# Stop everything, keep all data
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml down

# Shell inside the app container
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml exec cdt sh
```

> [!WARNING]
> `down -v` deletes the `postgres_data` and `minio_data` volumes — every organization, model,
> and uploaded file is gone with them. Only use it when you deliberately want a clean slate.

Application logs are also written to a `logs/` folder beside the compose file via a bind mount, so
they survive container restarts.

---

## Database access

Stock PostgreSQL 15 with PostGIS, so any Postgres client works. Nothing extra runs by default.

| | |
|---|---|
| Host / port | `localhost:5433` from your machine, `postgres:5432` inside the compose network |
| Credentials | `POSTGRES_DB` / `POSTGRES_USER` / `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` from your `.env` |

> [!WARNING]
> **On a server, close these ports first.** The compose file publishes Postgres (`5433`) and the
> MinIO console (`9001`) on *every* interface, so on a public host the database is reachable behind
> nothing but `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`. Prefix both with `127.0.0.1:` in the compose file, or firewall
> them, and connect through an SSH tunnel instead.

**SQL shell** — `psql` is already in the container:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml exec postgres sh -c 'psql -U $POSTGRES_USER -d $POSTGRES_DB'
```

The single quotes keep `$POSTGRES_USER` unexpanded so it resolves inside the container; this form
works in bash and PowerShell alike. Prisma creates tables in PascalCase, so quote them:
`select * from "User";` works, `select * from User;` does not.

**GUI clients** — point DBeaver, pgAdmin, TablePlus, or DataGrip at `localhost:5433`.

**Prisma Studio** — a schema-aware row browser, run only when you want it:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.public.yml run --rm -p 127.0.0.1:5555:5555 migrate yarn prisma studio --port 5555 --hostname 0.0.0.0 --browser none
```

Open http://localhost:5555; Ctrl-C stops it and `--rm` removes the container. It has no login and
full read/write on every table, `User` and `Account` included — keep the `127.0.0.1:` prefix and
reach it remotely over an SSH tunnel (`ssh -L 5555:localhost:5555 you@server`). Edits bypass the
app, so tenant scoping and the Postgres↔MinIO pairing are not enforced; treat it as read-mostly.

> [!NOTE]
> PowerShell: keep each command on one line (`\` continuations fail), and prefix with `--%` when a
> `-c "..."` payload contains quotes.

---

## Plugins

Plugins are **off by default**, and none are mounted by the compose file. A mounted plugin runs on
the main thread with the same access as CDT itself — there is no sandbox — so only mount plugins
you trust and have read.

To enable them, set `PLUGINS_ENABLED=true` in `.env`, create a `plugins/` folder beside the compose
file with one sub-folder per plugin (each containing a `manifest.json` and `dist/index.js`), and add
the read-only mount to the `cdt` service:

```yaml
    volumes:
      - ./logs:/app/logs
      - ./plugins:/app/plugins:ro
```

Read-only means a plugin cannot rewrite itself or its neighbours. Mounting one does not run it: an
administrator still has to add it on the **Plugins** page, and each user switches it on for
themselves. Full details in the
[plugin guide](https://docs.collabdt.org/docs/plugins/mounting-a-plugin/).

---

## Troubleshooting

**Uploads or downloads fail in the browser.** `MINIO_URL` must be a hostname the *browser* can
reach, because it is embedded in presigned URLs. `http://minio:9000` is the internal Docker
network name and will never resolve from a browser. Use `http://host.docker.internal:9000` on
Docker Desktop, your LAN IP on Linux, or your public hostname on a server.

**reCAPTCHA rejects login on localhost.** Add `localhost` as an allowed domain in the
[reCAPTCHA console](https://www.google.com/recaptcha/admin).

**Port already in use.** Something else holds 6012, 9000, 9001, or 5433. Free the port or change
the left-hand side of the mapping in the compose file.

**`migrate` exits with an error.** Check `DATABASE_URL` first — it must point at the `postgres`
service on the internal port (`postgresql://user:pass@postgres:5432/db`), not `localhost:5433`.

For DNS, TLS, reverse proxy, and multi-user deployment, see the
[full self-hosting guide](https://docs.collabdt.org/docs/deployment/self-hosting/).

